## Cases B and C: same veto, new physics (from `results/case_b`, `results/case_c`, regenerated by `make bench-steady`)
Both solve −∇·(k∇u) = f with u = 0 on the boundary (5-point finite volumes, harmonic face averaging, sparse
direct solve). The veto keeps the same scoring and swaps the energy check for a conservation check: the heat or
fluid leaving through the boundary must equal what the sources inject.

| Metric | Case B · heat | Case C · Darcy |
|---|---|---|
| FNO error, in-distribution (median relative L2) | {{b_fno_err}} | {{c_fno_err}} |
| U-Net error, in-distribution | {{b_unet_err}} | {{c_unet_err}} |
| Veto precision / recall ({{b_n_eval}} and {{c_n_eval}} predictions) | {{b_veto_p}} / {{b_veto_r}} | {{c_veto_p}} / {{c_veto_r}} |
| Recall counting VETO + REVIEW | {{b_flag_r}} | {{c_flag_r}} |
| Bad answers among accepted (vs. no veto) | {{b_acc_bad}} (vs. {{b_raw_bad}}) | {{c_acc_bad}} (vs. {{c_raw_bad}}) |
| Veto rate on normal in-distribution work | {{b_vr}} | {{c_vr}} |
| Sparse direct solver vs FNO batched, 64×64, one CPU thread | {{b_fd_ms}} ms vs {{b_fno_ms}} ms | {{c_fd_ms}} ms vs {{c_fno_ms}} ms |

What the per-mode tables show: in Case B the veto misses part of the uniform amplitude drift (a scaled
temperature field is still nearly smooth and balanced), and a few in-distribution predictions just over the 10%
tolerance are accepted. In Case C the FNO happens to generalize well to channels and fine-scale rock, but the
out-of-distribution check vetoes them anyway: conservative, it costs solver runs, not wrong answers.

