### Data-only vs physics-informed vs PINN (Case B)
{{pinn_table}}

Training minutes are wall-clock on a 4-core cloud CPU with several jobs running at once; compare them with each
other, not with other hardware. The physics-loss FNO adds the same discrete residual the veto checks to its training loss (PINO-style). The
classic PINN (MLP, autograd derivatives, no solver data) has to be trained again for every new board layout,
so it is a solver, not a surrogate. Here it is the most accurate per instance, but every answer costs a full training run
instead of milliseconds. With only 100 training samples the physics loss lowers both the FNO's error and its
residual (rows 2 and 4): that is the case for physics-informed training when solver data is scarce.

## Why this matters for CAE / HPC customers
- **Trust is the adoption blocker, not accuracy.** A per-prediction Trust Score with physical evidence lets
  engineers use surrogate answers where they are safe and know when they are not.
- **The veto rate is the business case.** Effective speedup = 1 / (veto rate + 1/speedup). A 10% veto rate
  caps the workflow at 10× no matter how fast the model is, so the ROI model subtracts every vetoed run.
- **It works with any surrogate.** The veto is a layer on top of existing solvers and model frameworks, so it
  fits into the tools customers already own.

## How it works
```mermaid
flowchart LR
    I["Input<br/>A: velocity model + shot<br/>B: conductivity + heat sources<br/>C: permeability + wells"] --> M["AI surrogates<br/>FNO + U-Net"]
    M --> V{"Physics veto<br/>PDE residual · boundary ·<br/>energy / conservation ·<br/>OOD · model disagreement"}
    V -- "ACCEPT" --> U["Use the answer"]
    V -- "REVIEW" --> E["Engineer review"]
    V -- "VETO" --> F["Full reference solver"]
    V --> R["ROI model<br/>net of vetoed runs"]
```
Each check is scored against its level on in-distribution validation data (`results/calibration.json`).
The weighted sub-scores give a **Trust Score (0-100)**; any check past its hard limit forces a VETO.
Weights, hard limits and verdict thresholds live in `config/veto.yaml` and `config/veto_steady.yaml`.
Details: [docs/ARCHITECTURE.md](docs/ARCHITECTURE.md).

